WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Good morning, Hank!

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:02.060
It’s Thursday.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.760
It’s Question Thursday, the day that I answer
real questions from real nerdfighters.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:06.830
Let’s get right to it.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.320
How’s the new book coming along?

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.350
In general, the less you hear me talking about
it, the better it’s going.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.580
Who do you look up to most in the world?

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:16.340
My parents, and then, secondarily, Dwayne
“The Rock” Johnson.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.380
How do you want to be remembered?

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.740
I’d like to be remembered mostly as a father
and a husband and a sponsor of AFC Wimbledon.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.720
What is your response to critics who say your
novels are pretentious?

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:25.560
Yeah.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.880
Do you have any advice for a decathlete?

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:30.820
Wait, are we talking about like running and
jumping decathlon or academic decathlon?

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.140
Because if it’s running and jumping decathlon,
I advise you to run fast and jump high.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.720
Or far. I don’t really know the rules.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.480
If we’re talking about academic decathlon,
I advise you to specialize in art history

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:41.480
and economics.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.400
Can you juggle?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.600
Yes, but only tasks.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.260
What’s the weirdest animal you’ve ever
met?

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.620
Probably Maureen Johnson.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.550
What John Green quote are you most proud of?

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.590
I’m pretty fond of “Imagining the future
is a kind of nostalgia,” but it’s not

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.780
actually my line.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.290
It’s something Sarah said to me on our first
date.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.470
And I was like, “Hold on a second,” and
I grabbed a napkin, and I wrote it down, and

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.380
I went home, and put it in Looking for Alaska.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.570
I did ask for her permission, for the record.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.630
How many copies of Harry Potter do you own?

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:09.550
We only have one copy of each book, except
for book four, which we have two copies of.

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.659
This one was on Quentin’s nightstand in
the Paper Towns movie.

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:16.209
If all the characters in your books ran for
President, which one would have your vote?

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.869
I feel like Hazel’s mom from The Fault in
Our Stars would be a pretty good president.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:22.859
You know, like, cool under pressure, thoughtful,
cautious, played by Laura Dern.

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:23.779
Favorite candy bar?

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.800
I mean, guys, come on, I’m literally sponsored
by Snickers.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.830
Favorite fictional place?

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:28.390
Hogwarts.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:29.400
Apple pie or pumpkin pie?

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.060
Pumpkin.

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.010
Favorite cheese?

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:31.510
Havarti.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:32.630
Go-to Chipotle order?

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.910
Barbacoa salad, no rice, black beans, extra
mild salsa, just a little bit of cheese, and

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.880
guacamole, which, yes, I know is extra.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.890
Favorite Pizza John design?

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.009
That’s like asking me to pick between my
children.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:43.669
I love them all differently, but equally.

00:01:43.669 --> 00:01:45.900
What should I get my girlfriend for our four-year
anniversary?

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:49.930
She already got a Pizzamas coffee mug for
our three-year anniversary on your recommendation.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.490
I got a list here of all the anniversary presents.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.590
It says the first year is paper, year fifty
is gold.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.479
It says here that year four is actually the
Pizza John hoodie.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.499
Will you mention The Road to Nerdfighteria?

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.600
Yeah, it’s this amazing project where nerdfighters
around the world talk about how they found

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.420
Nerdfighteria. Link in the dooblydoo.

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:06.800
Would you ever consider becoming President
of the United States?

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.790
No, I would be very bad at that job, and it
involves a lot of things I find very unpleasant,

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.900
like shaking hands with strangers and asking
rich people for money.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.420
John, why aren’t you Snapchatting?

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.860
So a few months ago, I turned my phone into
a dumb phone that does nothing but text, make

00:02:19.861 --> 00:02:21.920
phone calls, and play Pokémon Go.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.079
So I guess it’s not that dumb, but this
change in my life has been kind of wonderful,

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.779
even if I do miss Snapchat a little.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.560
How do you stay informed about current events?

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.460
This is another change I made about a year
ago.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.090
I read The Economist, The New York Times,
The Wall Street Journal, and my hometown paper,

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.130
The Indianapolis Star.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:39.040
I used to think that reading whatever was
on my social media feed was the best way to

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.870
stay informed, but now I’ve become a little
bit suspicious of that proposition.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.910
How do you feel about social media, anyway?

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.060
You know how sometimes you’re in a really
intense romantic relationship, and you can

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:51.229
kind of tell that the candle is burning at
both ends and will not last the night?

00:02:51.229 --> 00:02:54.060
And your friends are like, “Maybe you guys should
break up,” and you know maybe you should,

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.980
but you’re having a lot of fun, and it’s
not like you’re gonna get married, you know,

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.209
I mean, this isn’t serious, is it?

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:00.069
Yeah, kind of like that.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:01.280
Why do you read fiction?

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:02.140
Two reasons, really.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.280
First, for consolation.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.910
Like, I understand in the abstract that I
am not alone, but reading good fiction helps

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.260
me feel unalone in like the deepest ways.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.320
You know, it makes me feel like even my inexpressible
fears and demons don’t separate me from humanity.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.640
And then, secondly, I think it’s nice and
maybe even important to escape the prison

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.849
of your consciousness for a while and try
to live in someone else’s mind.

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:24.469
And for me, reading stories is like the most
efficient way out of myself.

00:03:24.469 --> 00:03:28.760
If I make my question a reminder that Pizzamas
ends tomorrow and all these amazing Pizzamas

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.240
wonders are only available for one more day
at dftba.com, will you make it the last question?

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.900
Yes. Yes I will.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.510
Hank, you’ll see me on Tuesday.

